I do remember that thread, and I think there’s a valuable interpretation of it.

If the final compiler is the reader, then the artifact is never truly complete until someone reconstructs it.

In that framing, the author doesn’t transmit a finished object. The author transmits a construction kit.

So instead of:

\text{Author}
\rightarrow
\text{Message}
\rightarrow
\text{Reader}

you get:

\text{Author}
\rightarrow
\text{Seed}
\rightarrow
\text{Reader}
\rightarrow
\text{Reconstruction}

The reader becomes an active participant in the computation.

That has a few implications:

* The artifact should contain enough structure to guide reconstruction.
* It should avoid over-specifying every detail, leaving room for the reader’s own knowledge and experience to complete the picture.
* It should preserve the invariants while allowing different valid instantiations.

Where I’d modify it slightly is this:

The author shouldn’t assume every reader will reconstruct the same thing.

Instead, the goal is to maximize the probability that independent readers converge on the important operators, even if their mental imagery, examples, or emotional responses differ.

That’s a stronger and more testable criterion.

In information-theoretic terms, you could say the artifact should maximize:

* semantic fidelity (the core ideas survive),
* reconstructability (the reader can rebuild the intended process),
* generativity (the reader can extend it),
* robustness (it survives different backgrounds and contexts).

So the compiler isn’t merely “the other reader.”

It’s the interaction between the artifact and the reader’s existing cognitive architecture.

The artifact supplies the seed.

The reader supplies prior knowledge, intuition, and experience.

The “compiled program” is the model that emerges in that reader’s mind.

That’s a powerful design goal because it shifts the focus away from writing something that is merely understood toward writing something that is successfully instantiated in another mind. The success of the artifact is measured not by how faithfully its words are repeated, but by whether it reliably generates the intended class of reasoning in people who encounter it.